  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 338 AM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThe long term picks up with the passage of a cold front through the \narea, ushering in the coolest air so far this fall from Thursday \nnight through the weekend. High pressure will continue to build \nbehind this front as a 1023mb surface high shifts southward out of \nCanada into the Midwest states by early next week. As a result, we \ncan expect very dry conditions to persist, with PW values less than \n0.25 inches and only a few passing clouds outside of the north \nGeorgia mountains on Friday. Surface winds will be elevated in the \npost frontal environment at around 20-25 mph through Friday \nafternoon, with some higher gusts up to 30 mph possible in the \nmountain counties. Otherwise, winds will lighten up somewhat over \nthe weekend as the gradient relaxes. \n\nAs mentioned, a few colder days can be expected, with daytime high \ntemperatures ranging from 8-10 degrees below average through the \nweekend. This will result in highs in the low to upper 50s warming \nas we progress into the first part of next week. Overnight lows will \ndrop to near or below freezing for the majority of our CWA, dipping \ninto the upper 20s across parts of north Georgia this weekend. We \nwill likely look to issue a Freeze Warning this weekend for counties \nexpecting below freezing temperatures. \n\nMoving into Monday, guidance highlights a shortwave trough pushing \nsoutheastward across Texas, with a possible developing coastal low \nmoving through the area by Tuesday. Guidance is not in agreement on \nwhether a low develops close enough to our area to bring meaningful \nrainfall, but if it does it likely will be a CAD-type event with \noverrunning showers and overcast conditions Monday night into \nTuesday with a minimal threat of severe weather.",
